# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# ...and we do opt in for the example: it is the only place the optional
# integration flags are documented, and `.env*` was silently keeping it out of
# every clone.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# local database
/.pglite/
/drizzle/meta/_journal.json.bak

# Captured real API responses (pnpm duffel:capture). Not committed: they are
# account-scoped, they carry real order ids, and the point of the harness is
# that anyone can regenerate them from their own test key. fixtures.ts holds
# the curated, committed payloads distilled from these.
/fixtures/live/

# The same, for pnpm salesforce:capture — and here the reason is stronger than
# account-scoping. These are records about real people at real companies. The
# capture script redacts emails, names and phone numbers before writing, and the
# conformance suite asserts that it did, but a belt-and-braces .gitignore is what
# stops a future capture step that forgets to redact from becoming a commit. §5j
# is exactly about a stranger's details not travelling somewhere they were never
# collected for, and a public git history is the least reversible such place.
/fixtures/live-salesforce/
